WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.790 --> 00:00:05.190 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  in the cold white north of minnesota  there's a place that will get you warm  after a long day on the ice  you can do some fishing  or have a tasty beverage  and it's something of a dream for josie  i've always wanted to own a neighborhood  bar and we finally do mine's just  sitting on a frozen lake  ice fishing is a way of life around here  for people  it gets so cold below zero attempts  quite often which allows us to drive and  put houses out and drill holes in the  lake and fish  when it gets cold enough in the early  winter  josie's husband goes out and tests the  ice to make sure it's thick enough  [Music]  if it is they can put the bar together  and then the ice hole can open  the ice here is so thick that this bar  sits on top of it  to come to our bar on the lake you just  drive your car out on the ice and park  we don't have a parking lot you just  park wherever you want walk in and have  a beer  people like to fish and drink beer and  from our bar here you can do that all at  the same time  the local favorite drink here is called  the minnow shot people will actually  take a live minnow which is fishing bait  and put it into their shop and drink it  [Music]  oh yeah  as delicious as that seafood beverage  sounds people come just because the fact  that the bar exists is pretty amazing  people's reactions to this bar are from  crazy to they don't understand it that's  the coolest thing ever they come from  all over the world to see it it's very  seasonal we're only open 20 days  if you're ever near lake lida drive out  on the ice park come into the bar and  grab a pole  [Music]  water is just such an amazing medium  you can just see the crystals and how  they form in so many different ways and  then take that and extrapolate it to  these huge cliffs  what we do here is create spray ice and  probably make over 300 different climbs  [Music]  my name is alexander bianchi and i'm an  ice farmer we're down here in the  uncompacted gorge  we have a water system at the top of the  gorge  these galvanized steel shower heads  and it basically just sprays water out  over the cliffs and overnight when it's  cold it forms ice along the cliffs  what we really want is a nice clean  surface for the new water to come down  and just keep building off of that so a  lot of what we do here actually is repel  down a lot of these faces and just try  to clear that snow off ready for the  next layer  there's about two miles of ice if you  walk the bottom of the canyon and uh  yeah we farm ice all along the top of it  being an ice farmer is kind of hard work  we're gonna open up the south valve  sometimes i think we're no more than  like glorified plumbers  ice farming is art and engineering i  mean the two i think are always really  inseparable you know the engineering is  the logistics how do we get water from  point a to point b sometimes if the  valves aren't cracked quite enough you  get  a little bit of a freeze up the water  just doesn't get to the valve the remedy  is a blowtorch  artistic side is once we have this water  where we need it how can we do something  different how can we make something  interesting that you know we've never  climbed before it's definitely an  artistic process for some of us  i grew up rock climbing you know fair  weather climber first time i came out  here  was just kind of blown away it's so  different than rock climbing you're  climbing a transient medium the rock is  always going to be there this this ice  is not always there so it creates this  diversity and  this intense experience that i really  don't find in other facets of climate  and that's why i'm proud to do what i'm  doing  making drawings on land on quite a large  scale is something people can do for  thousands of years  [Music]  i'm simon beck  and i make drawings in the snow  [Music]  it started as a bit of fun  but gradually he's taken over my life  a ballpark estimate of footsteps  about 5 000 steps in an hour  a big drawing  two hours measuring eight hours work  you're looking at forty thousand steps  [Music]  i started doing it as an easy  alternative to hiking at the mountain  when i wanted an exercise  [Music]  you're starting with a drawer on a piece  of paper  or else to draw it in your mind  just treating it a bit like an  orienteering map  you're just going a certain distance in  a certain direction to determine  distances by counting bases  it's a very physical exercise it's  equivalent to a long days walking in the  hills when you make one of these big  drawings  [Music]  i'm just about the only person in the  world doing anything like it  [Music]  [Music]  this incredible colorful city  will only last a few months  but for one local designer the seasonal  spectacular is worth it  [Music]  jin zhang is an award-winning ice  sculptor and art teacher in harbin china  carbine is known as the city of ice and  each year hosts the incredible spectacle  the harbin snow and ice festival  these large scale designs are remarkable  but it's quite a process  one jinan knows well  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  starting with a block of plain ice may  seem daunting but to jin en this is just  the beginning  [Music]  of  foreign  but the reality of working with a  seasonal medium has its downfalls  foreign  [Music]  [Music]  it's obviously an icon  for the mountain  just the symbol of it  [Music]  you don't have a lot of opportunity in  life these days  to have 10 or 12 minutes  [Music]  alone  [Music]  some people think  when they come here and they ride the  chair it's a lonely ride  i never really thought of it that way  mad river was founded by a guy named  roland palmetto because he wanted to  start a ski area where sport and not  money was going to be the overriding  concern he built the single chair here  in 1948  and then someone figured out very  quickly that a double chair was just as  easy to build as a single chair the  single chairs went away very quickly and  this thing has kind of hung around for a  long time  you're forced to really focus on what's  going on around you to look down just  watch some of the skiers having fun and  when you get up there you can look  across the valley into new york  [Music]  as the ski industry has changed over the  years this place hasn't everything that  we do  shouldn't work but it does  there's not a lot of ski areas in the  world where  the skiers are donating money to build a  lift  there's something about like you know  waiting in line in the corral and you're  going back and forth  and you're saying hi to people you're  carrying on multiple conversations  it's just a real friendly vibe  that you know it's not like a hashtag  campaign where we're trying to  create a community it's already here  sitting on that single chair  is like coming home  [Music]

